# PLAN-1b: Fix CPU dtype crash + persist counters, re-run the smoke gate (still gating Phase-1)

## Current evidence (verified in PLAN-1)
- `full_e_hsfp` smoke ran clean (2 rounds, finite losses) with NONZERO real counters: memory
  812/436/20, reliability nonuniform (461 calls), PRC loss >0 (564 nonzero calls), distinct hash +
  output dir. → Phase-0 fixes fire on real data.
- `baseline_hsfp` CRASHED at `classification/H-SFP/hierarchy.py:781`: CPU `bfloat16` input vs FP32
  convolution dtype mismatch. Routine bug, not a scientific signal.
- The smoke ran on CPU (514s for a tiny 8-client/2-round run) → a real grid on CPU is infeasible.
- Host has CUDA: RTX 3080 Ti, 12 GB (≈4 GB already used by an unrelated process → ≈8 GB free).
- Baseline runtime counters were absent because the crash happened before metrics were persisted;
  exact memory read/write, reliability variance scalar, PRC scalar, and dropout active-N are not
  yet persisted per-round.

## Hypothesis
The failure is purely a device/dtype plumbing issue: (a) running on CPU triggers a bfloat16-input
/ FP32-conv mismatch that GPU + a defensive dtype cast avoids; (b) counters are simply not flushed
per-round. Fixing both lets BOTH presets complete with finite losses and emit distinct, nonzero,
differing counters — passing the Phase-1 gate.

## Proposed isolated change
Minimal: (1) route smoke/training to CUDA when available; make autocast/bfloat16 conditional on a
supported device and ensure conv inputs match weight dtype (defensive cast) so CPU also does not
crash; (2) persist per-round runtime counters incrementally for BOTH presets so a crash still
leaves partial counters. No algorithmic/scientific change.

## Invariants
- No training grid / full schedule. Same bounded smoke budget as PLAN-1.
- Respect the ~12 GB GPU (≈8 GB free) budget; do not OOM the co-resident process.
- Preserve uncommitted user changes and all existing results; unique smoke output dirs.
- No sample-level activation/gradient transfer; local only (no push/PR/network-external).

